---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseStudyProse from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyProse.astro";
import CaseStudySection from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudySection.astro";
import CaseStudyCards from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyCards.astro";
import CaseStudyCard from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyCard.astro";

import heroImage from "../../assets/images/woven-and-woods.png";
import catalogueImage from "../../assets/images/woven-and-woods/catalogue.png";
import rangeImage from "../../assets/images/woven-and-woods/range.png";
import quizImage from "../../assets/images/woven-and-woods/quiz.png";
import settingsImage from "../../assets/images/woven-and-woods/theme-settings.png";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
	title="Rebuilding the Woven & Woods website"
	pageTitle="Woven & Woods website case study - Will Jackson"
	description="How I rebuilt the Woven & Woods website on a custom WordPress block theme, with around 40 bespoke blocks, structured content and search protected through the move."
	path="/work/woven-and-woods/"
	lede="A custom WordPress build for an independent flooring retailer, designed to make a large catalogue easy to browse, get customers into the showrooms and let staff run the site themselves."
	facts={[
		{ label: "Client", value: "Woven & Woods, flooring showrooms in Twickenham and Richmond" },
		{ label: "Role", value: "Development, content structure, SEO and deployment" },
		{ label: "Stack", value: "WordPress block theme, ACF, custom plugins, Rank Math PRO, Cloudflare" },
		{ label: "Year", value: "2026" },
	]}
	heroImage={heroImage}
	heroImageAlt="The new Woven & Woods homepage"
	link="https://wovenandwoods.com"
>
	<CaseStudyProse heading="The brief">
		<p>
			I'd built and run the previous Woven & Woods site, a bespoke
			WordPress and WooCommerce theme that grew organic search traffic by
			95% over three years. But the business had outgrown it: a second
			showroom, a much bigger catalogue, and a team who needed to change
			things without waiting for me.
		</p>
		<p>
			The new site had four jobs: make the whole catalogue easy to browse,
			turn browsing into showroom visits, keep the search rankings the old
			site had earned, and be simple for non-technical staff to manage.
		</p>
	</CaseStudyProse>

	<CaseStudySection
		heading="A catalogue built from custom blocks"
		image={catalogueImage}
		imageAlt="The range catalogue, with category tiles, brand and price filters and range cards"
	>
		<p>
			Most of the site runs on two custom plugins. The first adds around
			40 bespoke blocks, from the range catalogue and its filters to
			showroom pages with live opening status and the case study listings.
			The second handles core features such as branded product URLs and
			enquiry emails.
		</p>
		<p>
			The catalogue lets customers narrow the whole collection by
			category, brand, price and whether a range is on display in a
			showroom, which is usually the question that decides whether they
			visit.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection
		heading="Structured content, not page layouts"
		image={rangeImage}
		imageAlt="A wood flooring range page showing category, format, showroom availability and size"
		reverse
	>
		<p>
			Content is modelled in Advanced Custom Fields as ranges, colourways,
			brands, showrooms, case studies and team members, rather than built
			page by page. Each range page is assembled from that data, so details
			like format, size and which showroom it's in stay consistent
			everywhere they appear.
		</p>
		<p>
			The catalogue was imported in bulk with WP All Import, and product
			images are served through Cloudflare's image CDN.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection
		heading="Helping customers choose"
		image={quizImage}
		imageAlt="The Find your floor quiz, asking which room the flooring is for"
	>
		<p>
			Flooring is a big decision and most customers don't know where to
			start, so the “Find your floor” quiz asks four quick questions, then
			shows a room the team has fitted and the ranges they'd recommend.
		</p>
		<p>
			From there, Fluent Forms and Fluent Booking handle enquiries and
			showroom appointments, and Google reviews are pulled in through
			Trustindex.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection
		heading="Built for the people who run it"
		image={settingsImage}
		imageAlt="The Theme Settings screen in WordPress, with tabs for each section of the site"
		reverse
	>
		<p>
			The site's design system (colour roles, a type scale and spacing)
			lives in theme.json, so the WordPress editor uses the brand's styles
			too and new pages look right by default.
		</p>
		<p>
			Site-wide copy, featured showrooms and featured case studies are
			edited in a Theme Settings screen, so staff can update the site
			without a developer.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudyCards>
		<CaseStudyCard heading="Protecting search through the move">
			<p>
				Rank Math PRO handles titles, sitemaps, breadcrumbs and redirects
				from the old site. Custom structured data marks up showrooms as
				local businesses, ranges as products, case studies as articles
				and FAQ sections as FAQs. An llms.txt file helps AI assistants
				describe the business accurately, and analytics run through
				Google Tag Manager.
			</p>
		</CaseStudyCard>

		<CaseStudyCard heading="How it's built and shipped">
			<p>
				Development happens locally in DDEV, with PHPUnit tests. Code
				lives in GitHub, and GitHub Actions deploys every change to the
				staging site automatically.
			</p>
		</CaseStudyCard>
	</CaseStudyCards>
</CaseStudyLayout>
